<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quality of 3D models.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the development of 3D generative models, the quality of 3D assets has been given more emphasis. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> evaluates the complexity of 3D mesh based on the number of salient edges. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> leverages a pretrained image aesthetic model<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            https://github.com/christophschuhmann/improved-aesthetic-predictor</span></span></span> to evaluate the rendering of 3D assets. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> introduces Objaverse++, which manually annotates 10K Objaverse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> assets with multi-view quality/aesthetic scores and attribute tags, then trains a predictor to scale these annotations to a curated subset of roughly 500K objects. They further show that filtering training data by these quality labels can improve image-to-3D generation performance and speed up training convergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>ELBO of Discrete Diffusion Models</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For USDMs, <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> from equation <a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Discrete diffusion models. ‣ 3.1 Generation Pipeline ‣ 3 Method ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is usually written as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> :</p>
<table id="A2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}(\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),\alpha_{t};\mathbf{x}_{0})=-\frac{\alpha_{t}^{\prime}}{K\alpha_{t}}\left[\frac{K}{\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}-\frac{K}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}}-\sum_{j}\frac{\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{j}}{\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{j}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{j}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>K</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}(\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),\alpha_{t};\mathbf{x}_{0})=-\frac{\alpha_{t}^{\prime}}{K\alpha_{t}}\left[\frac{K}{\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}-\frac{K}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}}-\sum_{j}\frac{\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{j}}{\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{j}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{j}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{x}}=K\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{0}+(1-\alpha_{t})\mathbf{1},\;\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}=K\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)+(1-\alpha_{t})\mathbf{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{x}}=K\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{0}+(1-\alpha_{t})\mathbf{1},\;\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}=K\alpha_{t}\mathbf{x}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)+(1-\alpha_{t})\mathbf{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the time derivative of <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=\arg\max_{j\in{K}}(\mathbf{x}_{t})_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=\arg\max_{j\in{K}}(\mathbf{x}_{t})_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as the non-zero entry of <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here we adopted its Rao Blackwellized Version as used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),\alpha_{t};\mathbf{x}_{0})=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(\mathbf{x}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t),\alpha_{t};\mathbf{x}_{0})=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle-\frac{\alpha_{t}^{\prime}}{K\alpha_{t}}\Bigl[\frac{K}{\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}-\frac{K}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}}-(\kappa_{t}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{0}}+\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}\neq\mathbf{x}_{0})})\sum_{j}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{j}}-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>K</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\frac{\alpha_{t}^{\prime}}{K\alpha_{t}}\Bigl[\frac{K}{\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}-\frac{K}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}}-(\kappa_{t}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{0}}+\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}\neq\mathbf{x}_{0})})\sum_{j}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{j}}-</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle K\frac{\alpha_{t}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{m}}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}\neq\mathbf{x}_{0}}-\left((K-1)\kappa_{t}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{0}}-\frac{1}{\kappa_{t}}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}\neq\mathbf{x}_{0}}\right)\log\kappa_{t}\Bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.167em">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K\frac{\alpha_{t}}{1-\alpha_{t}}\log\frac{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{i}}{(\bar{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta})_{m}}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}\neq\mathbf{x}_{0}}-\left((K-1)\kappa_{t}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}=\mathbf{x}_{0}}-\frac{1}{\kappa_{t}}\mathbbm{1}_{\mathbf{x}_{t}\neq\mathbf{x}_{0}}\right)\log\kappa_{t}\Bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{t}=(1-\alpha_{t})/(K\alpha_{t}+1-\alpha_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{t}=(1-\alpha_{t})/(K\alpha_{t}+1-\alpha_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> denote the index where <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{m}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{m}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Emperically, this works better than cross entropy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Prior of Discrete Diffusion Models</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Generation Pipeline ‣ 3 Method ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the prior distribution of USDMs is <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}=\mathbf{1}/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}=\mathbf{1}/K</annotation></semantics></math>, which means an equal probability of all states. For MDMs, the prior distribution <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\pi}=\mathbf{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝅</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\pi}=\mathbf{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a degenerated distribution with probability 1 on the special MASK token. Despite the difference in prior distribution, these two frameworks demonstrate different behaviors during the diffusion process. In USDMs, each categorical latent can either stay the same or transition uniformly to any other category in
<math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the transition probabilities controlled by the diffusion timestep. In contrast, MDMs allow a token to either remain as a mask or to be revealed to other tokens, and once revealed, it stays masked throughout the reverse process. In other words, if MDMs make a mistake, there is no chance to fix them.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During our early experiment, we found that USDM outperforms MDM even in an early stage, where USDM starts to generate image-aligned voxels, while MDM still struggles to generate meaningful objects, as shown in Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Prior of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>; thus, we decided to use USDMs for this task.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><img src="2605.07971v1/figures/mdmudlm/image-step60000-4088aa8ed2140fc3.png" id="A3.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/143;" width="143" height="143" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><img src="2605.07971v1/figures/mdmudlm/mdm.png" id="A3.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/143;" width="143" height="143" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3"><img src="2605.07971v1/figures/mdmudlm/udlm.png" id="A3.F7.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:143/143;" width="143" height="143" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Example of the generation result of USDM and MDM at 60K steps of training, with ancestral sampler. CFG set to 2.0 and NFE=256. <span id="A3.F7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>: Condition Image. <span id="A3.F7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Middle</span>: MDM generated voxel. <span id="A3.F7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>: USDM generated voxel.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main reason for the degraded performance of MDMs, we suspect, is the sequence length. In our work, we use voxel grids with a resolution of 64, yielding sequences of <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\approx</annotation></semantics></math>200K
tokens. This creates significant difficulty for MDMs: during each sampling step, thousands of tokens may be revealed at once, which leads to a high likelihood of early-stage errors. From Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Generation Dynamics and Uncertainty Assesment ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, it can be seen that in early sampling stages, the model is less certain about the occupancy of voxels, which further amplifies the chances of making early mistakes. These errors then accumulate through the reverse process and ultimately degrade generation quality. We tried to leverage several inference time scaling techniques for correcting MDMs, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. While these methods demonstrate improvements compared to pure MDM settings, USDM still presents better sample quality without introducing additional computations.
The intrinsic self-correcting behavior of USDMs makes them better suited for handling such long-sequence but simple states settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Design Space of Discrete Diffusion Models</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the lack of a general voxel perceptual model, it can be difficult and expensive to evaluate the performance of a voxel generation model. Thus, we leverage a simpler surrogate dataset, MNIST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, to provide partial information for different settings as a reference. For all the tests, we discretize the MNIST images into 8 categories, according to their pixel values. We train a tiny DiT (around 6M parameters) on the datasets for 50k steps under different settings, and we evaluate its FIDs over 10k generated images and likelihood on the validation set. The images are generated with 256 denoising steps. With the MNIST result as a reference, we also conduct several ablation studies on a confidential inner dataset (INNER3D for short). For evaluation, we select 20K meshes from INNER3D randomly as the validation set. We train a small DiT model (<math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>140M params) for 140K steps with AdamW optimizer with no weight decay and <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr=1e-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr=1e-4</annotation></semantics></math>. Evaluate the negative log likelihood under the EMA 0.9999 weights for experiments with the INNER3D dataset. The exploration of design spaces is done with image-conditioned generation tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Sampling Distribution of <math id="A4.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the ELBO objective <a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Discrete diffusion models. ‣ 3.1 Generation Pipeline ‣ 3 Method ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We tested distributions that focus on different time sampling intervals on discretized MNIST, including: uniform distribution, logit Normal distributions, and Beta distributions, as shown in Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Training. ‣ D.1 Sampling Distribution of 𝑡 ‣ Appendix D Design Space of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and Table <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Training. ‣ D.1 Sampling Distribution of 𝑡 ‣ Appendix D Design Space of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F9.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07971v1/PDFDifferentDistributions.svg" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:229/164;" width="229" height="164"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>PDF of different distributions.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A4.F9.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>NLL and FID results on discretized MNIST.</figcaption>
