</table>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Training. ‣ D.1 Sampling Distribution of 𝑡 ‣ Appendix D Design Space of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> suggests that more training steps around <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> might potentially lead to better results.
For further investigation on the 3D task, we choose logit Normal <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, logit Normal <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and Beta<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> as our candidate distributions, with different focuses on time intervals. We evaluate the negative log-likelihood at 140K steps of training on the INNER3D dataset in Table <a href="#A4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Training. ‣ D.1 Sampling Distribution of 𝑡 ‣ Appendix D Design Space of Discrete Diffusion Models ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>The evaluation negative log-likelihood on INNER3D</figcaption>
<table id="A4.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A4.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Dist.</td>
<td id="A4.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">NLL <math id="A4.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">LogitNormal(0,1)</td>
<td id="A4.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.01369</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">LogitNormal(1,1)</td>
<td id="A4.T4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.01457</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T4.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T4.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Beta(3,1)</td>
<td id="A4.T4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.01341</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although it suggests that Beta<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> might be the best candidate distribution, we found that as training continues, both logit Normal <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and Beta<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> exhibit unstable sampling behavior and degraded quality, posing extra difficulties in CFG tuning. Thus, we choose logit Normal <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> for image generation tasks. For text-conditioned generation tasks, we found that the Beta<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> reports a better metric and generation result, possibly due to text-conditioned generation being a more difficult task. So for text-conditioned models, we choose Beta<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Structural Design</h3>

<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Positional embedding.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While RoPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> has been a standard technique in language modeling, its relative attention nature makes it also a good fit in 3D generative modeling. We conduct an ablation study in both absolute positional embedding (APE) and RoPE. By default, ROPE is only performed on the self-attention part, but not on the cross-attention part. We think the absolute position is also an important feature in 3D generative modeling, especially for image conditioning; thus, we optionally add APE in the cross-attention part to make cross-attention have access to local information. For all the positional embeddings, we keep the t sampling distribution to logit-Normal<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we also choose RoPE with absolute positional embedding for our final version model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Evaluated negative log-likelihood of the validation set from INNER3D.</figcaption>
<table id="A4.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A4.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Model</td>
<td id="A4.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">NLL <math id="A4.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">APE</td>
<td id="A4.T5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.01457</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">RoPE</td>
<td id="A4.T5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.01451</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T5.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T5.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">APE+RoPE</td>
<td id="A4.T5.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A4.T5.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.01448</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stabilized training.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During our experiments, we have observed undesirable training dynamics: the grad norm will stop decreasing at some point around (250K steps), and start to increase with occasional spikes. Keep training will, counterintuitively, produce lower negative log-likelihood but, subsequently, worse generation quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Generation Pipeline ‣ 3 Method ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the model output
<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the unnormalized log-probabilities
(logits) of the discrete states. During training, as the model becomes increasingly
confident, these logits may grow to extremely large magnitudes, leading to numerical
instability and undesirable optimization behavior. For instance, we observed the sampling processes where a normalized log-probability reached <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-33" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>33</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-33</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to a
probability of approximately <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-15}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-15}</annotation></semantics></math>. Such over-sharpened logits can destabilize the training
dynamics and ultimately degrade sampling quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate this issue, we introduce a centered and <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>tanh</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh</annotation></semantics></math>-truncated logit parameterization, specifically designed for binary (two-state) discrete diffusion models. Concretely, we first center the logits by subtracting their mean, thereby removing any uninformative global shift. We then apply a scaled <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>tanh</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh</annotation></semantics></math> function with parameter <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, which softly bounds the logit differences and prevents the model from producing excessively confident predictions. This modification stabilizes both training and sampling without changing the underlying discrete diffusion objective.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Centered and <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>tanh</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh</annotation></semantics></math>-Truncated Logits</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
                
                
               Raw logits from the model: <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{raw}}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>raw</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{raw}}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
                
                
               Saturation parameter <math id="alg1.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{centered}}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}_{\text{raw}}-\operatorname{mean}(\mathbf{x}_{\text{raw}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>centered</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>raw</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>mean</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>raw</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{centered}}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}_{\text{raw}}-\operatorname{mean}(\mathbf{x}_{\text{raw}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{saturated}}\leftarrow\tau\cdot\tanh\!\left(\frac{\mathbf{x}_{\text{centered}}}{\tau}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>saturated</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.896em;" width="1.896em"><mi>tanh</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>centered</mtext></msub><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{saturated}}\leftarrow\tau\cdot\tanh\!\left(\frac{\mathbf{x}_{\text{centered}}}{\tau}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
               <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{logits}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}_{\text{saturated}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>logits</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>saturated</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{logits}\leftarrow\mathbf{x}_{\text{saturated}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3a" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
               logits

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We choose the truncate parameter <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=5</annotation></semantics></math>, which approximately corresponds to a limit probability of <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5e-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5e-5</annotation></semantics></math>. We found that this parametrization could significantly stabilize both training and sampling behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Abandon time conditioning.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In MDMs, people usually drop the time-step conditioning in the model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, since the number of mask tokens can itself be an indicator of the current time-step. In continuous diffusion models, some works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite> also studied the necessity of time conditioning.
In USDMs, the time step is also inherited in the chaoticity of the input, though less straightforward than MDMs. In experiments of MNIST, we found that removing the time-step conditioning, while slightly harming the NLL, demonstrates better sampling quality:</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>MNIST experiments with and without time conditioning.</figcaption>
<table id="A4.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A4.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Setting</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">NLL</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">FID</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T6.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T6.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Uniform</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.2816</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">11.35</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T6.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T6.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Uniform w/o <math id="A4.T6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.2861</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">7.11</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T6.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T6.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">logitNormal(1,1)</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.2787</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">3.94</td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T6.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T6.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">logitNormal(1,1) w/o <math id="A4.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.2807</td>
<td id="A4.T6.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">3.74</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While we keep time conditioning in our generation model to ensure minimal variation from typical of USDMs, we drop it for our fine-tuning tasks in Section. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Block-Structured Perturbations for Fine-tuning ‣ 3 Method ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> by setting the time step to <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. This also simplifies the inpainting algorithm since we don’t need to calculate the actual time <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> every step.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>More Implementation Details</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Training</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the design choices mentioned above, we also incorporate a cosine learning rate decay schedule from HuggingFace transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> with a warm-up step of 5000. The training and fine-tuning are conducted on 64 NVIDIA A800 GPUs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Fine-tuning</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Even though we tried to keep the block structures in a fraction of a predefined <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix H Perturbing and Inpainting Algorithm ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), the actual perturbed region still varies because of rounding errors and overlapping. We present the distribution of the occupancy ratio of the generated blocks and the background <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> grid when <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07971v1/PerturbHistogram.svg" id="A5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/165;" width="238" height="165"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>The Ratio of Generated Block Structure and Background Grid. The perturbation fraction <math id="A5.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}\sim\mathcal{U}[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During fine-tuning, we replace half of the perturbation strategy to BSP with uniformly distributed <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>. Within the blocked structures, we apply the forward diffusion kernel with <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\rm{Beta}(3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\rm{Beta}(3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> to keep the focus on larger perturbation portions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Quantitative Results</h3>

<section id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generation.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of rendering a depth image and unprojecting, we directly normalize and sample 50k points from the surface for the generated mesh and the GT mesh, and compute the averaged Chamfer distance. By this, we evaluate the geometry quality of the whole mesh instead of only the visible part. For image conditioning, we select the image with a pitch angle of 30 degrees, a yaw angle of 20 degrees, and a radius of 2.5. For computing the metric for TRELLIS, we sample voxels with CFG strength as 3 and a sampling step of 50, being consistent with the original paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. For the second stage, we stick with the default setting of TRELLIS’s official repo: for the image conditioned task, the CFG strength is set to 5.0 for <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0.5,1.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0.5,1.0]</annotation></semantics></math> and for the text conditioned task, the CFG is set to <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.5</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0.5,0.95]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0.5,0.95]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since we have observed that there are misalignment cases in GT meshes and generated meshes, possibly due to the diversity of alignment in the training set and differences in data processing conventions, we pre-align all generated voxels with the GT mesh before we pass the generated sparse voxels to the second stage. For TRELLIs and ours method, we first produce the cubified mesh for the generated voxel, and we compute the chamfer distance between the normalized cubified mesh and the GT mesh with 10K points across 24 possible poses (6 oriented axes with 4 different up directions), then we rotate the voxels to the pose with the minimal chamfer distance. We found that this method aligns most of the meshes to the correct direction, which gives us more reliable geometric metrics. To conduct experiments on Direct3D-S2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and TripoSR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, we use their official implementations and released checkpoints (<a href="https://github.com/DreamTechAI/Direct3D-S2" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Direct3D-S2</a>
, <a href="https://github.com/VAST-AI-Research/TripoSR" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">TripoSR</a>
). For Direct3D-S2, since one of the outputs is a latent index representing voxel coordinates used in the second-stage generation, we store it together with the final mesh. We observe that the generated meshes follow a canonical orientation aligned with one of the six principal axes; therefore, we align each mesh to the ground truth by selecting the axis permutation that minimizes the Chamfer distance. For TripoSR, the generated meshes also exhibit a consistent canonical orientation. We apply a sequence of rotations (<math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-150^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>150</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-150^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> about the X-axis, followed by <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-60^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-60^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> about the Y-axis, and <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>65</mn><mo>∘</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> about the Z-axis) to align all meshes such that they face the positive z-axis, with the positive y-axis defined as the upward direction. We then apply our voxelization method on the aligned meshes to obtain corresponding voxel representations. All metrics are computed on the aligned meshes and their corresponding aligned voxel representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We present the detailed implementation of the evaluation pipeline.</p>
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The point cloud FID following PointNet++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. We sample 4000 points from each mesh and compute the P-FID, denoted as <math id="A5.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FID}_{\text{PC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FID</mtext><mtext>PC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FID}_{\text{PC}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the FID of Voxels, we sample 4000 points from the cubified mesh of the GT voxel and the generated voxels. For computing the chamfer distance, we sample 10000 points from each mesh.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The FID under DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> feature spaces, where we render 6 images per asset with yaw angles at every 60 degrees, a pitch angle of 30 degrees, and a radius of 2.5. Totally, there are 15000 images in the training subset and 6000 images in the Toys4k evaluation set.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also compute the CLIP score of rendered results and the GT rendering image set. We render 6 images per generated asset with yaw angles at every 60 degrees, a pitch angle of 30 degrees, and a radius of 2.5, and calculate the maximal CLIP score across these 6 images per asset, then report the averaged CLIP score across all assets.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Uncertainty scores</h4>

<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The distribution of uncertainty scores of the training set is given in Figure <a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Uncertainty scores ‣ E.3 Quantitative Results ‣ Appendix E More Implementation Details ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, several example meshes with their <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> scores are shown in Figure <a href="#A5.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Uncertainty scores ‣ E.3 Quantitative Results ‣ Appendix E More Implementation Details ‣ DVD: Discrete Voxel Diffusion for 3D Generation and Editing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07971v1/complexity_histogram.svg" id="A5.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/123;" width="238" height="123"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>Complexity Scores <math id="A5.F11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of TRELLIS500K Dataset</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A5.F12" class="ltx_figure">
